%[text] # comp.FrontLoadedHorn Class
%[text] **J.G. Vermond, JVsound**
%[text] Front-loaded horn: the front of the diaphragm drives a horn through a throat chamber, the rear works on a closed chamber.
%[text] **Namespace:** comp
%[text] **Superclasses:** [comp.Enclosure](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','comp','enclosure','enclosuredoc.m')))
%%
%[text] ## Description
%[text] **Draft.** This page is a first design of the acoustical model; the class is not implemented yet. The `comp.FrontLoadedHorn` class will model a front-loaded horn: the front of the diaphragm of the driver in `Driver` works through a throat chamber into a horn, which radiates at its mouth; the rear works on a closed chamber. For an FEA model of such an enclosure, see [`comp.FeaEnclosure`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','comp','feaenclosure','feaenclosuredoc.m'))).
%%
%[text] ### Acoustical model
%[text] The acoustical side is the two-port network $ \\mathbf{T}\_a $ with the 4-port $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $ of the enclosure and the 4-port $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,rad} $ of the radiation, on the front conductor (top), the ambient pressure (middle, the reference) and the rear conductor (bottom), as for [`comp.ClosedBox`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','comp','closedbox','closedboxdoc.m'))):
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:f1h7)
%[text] - $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $, rear conductor: the acoustic mass $ M\_a = \\frac{M\_{mi}}{S\_d^2} $ of the air that moves with the rear of the diaphragm in series, and the compliance $ C\_{a1} = \\frac{V\_r}{\\rho c^2} $ of the rear chamber to the ambient pressure, as in a closed box, with the losses of the rear chamber: the leakage resistance $ R\_{a1} $ as a shunt to the ambient pressure and the absorption resistance $ R\_{a2} $ (damping material) in series with $ C\_{a1} $.
%[text] - $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $, front conductor: the compliance $ C\_{a2} = \\frac{V\_t}{\\rho c^2} $ of the throat chamber to the ambient pressure, then the horn as a 2-port $ \\mathbf{T}\_h $ between the front conductor and the reference, from the throat to the mouth.
%[text] - $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,rad} $: the radiation impedance $ Z\_{rad} $ of the mouth on the front conductor; as a first model a rigid piston in an infinite baffle, `zarad` with the radius $ r\_m $ of the mouth. The rear conductor passes through. \
%[text] As a first model, the horn is a chain of $ N $ short segments of length $ \\Delta x $, each a uniform tube with the area $ S\_i $ of the horn profile at its middle, so that any profile (conical, exponential, tractrix) can be used:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\mathbf{T}\_h = \\prod\_{i=1}^{N} \\left\[ \\matrix{\\cos k \\Delta x & j \\frac{\\rho c}{S\_i} \\sin k \\Delta x \\cr j \\frac{S\_i}{\\rho c} \\sin k \\Delta x & \\cos k \\Delta x} \\right\] $
%[text] **Open points of the design:** the horn profile and how it is given (a flare law or a table of areas), the number of segments, the air load on the front of the diaphragm (the driver takes it out of $ M\_{md} $, and the throat chamber does not add it back), how the losses $ R\_{a1} $ and $ R\_{a2} $ are given (quality factors or resistances, and their defaults), and the losses in the horn, which would be part of $ \\mathbf{T}\_h $.
%%
%[text] ## Creation
%[text] Not implemented yet. The planned syntax is `obj = comp.FrontLoadedHorn`, which creates a front-loaded horn with default dimensions and a default driver.
%%
%[text] ## Properties
%[text] Planned properties (not implemented yet):
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[200,120,-1]}
%[text] | Property | Symbol | Description |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `RearVolume` | $ V\_r $ | Volume of the rear chamber in m3 |
%[text] | `ThroatChamberVolume` | $ V\_t $ | Volume of the throat chamber in m3 |
%[text] | `ThroatArea` | $ S\_t $ | Area of the horn at the throat in m2 |
%[text] | `MouthArea` | $ S\_m $ | Area of the horn at the mouth in m2 |
%[text] | `HornLength` | $ L\_h $ | Length of the horn in m |
%[text] | `Driver` | | Driver that is mounted in the enclosure, inherited from `comp.Enclosure` |
%[text:table]
%%
%[text] ## Methods
%[text] Planned: `tae` and `tarad` with the elements above; `zaFront`, `zaRear` and `micTransfer` are inherited from `comp.Enclosure`, like `zarad` and `struve`. See [`solve2PortNetwork`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','lspsys','methods','solve2portnetworkdoc','solve2portnetworkdoc.m'))) for how the 4-ports are reduced.

